# gitleaks — triaged historical findings
#
# One line per finding fingerprint (commit:file:rule:startline). A fingerprint
# names a finding; it carries no secret material. Everything not listed here is
# still scanned at full strength, in the working tree and in every commit — this
# file suppresses the named finding and nothing else.
#
# Why this exists: `gitleaks git` re-scans the entire history on every push, so a
# finding that is already in history is reported forever, even after the code is
# reverted. Triaging it once here is gitleaks' own documented mechanism for that
# (README, "ignore specific findings"). Deleting a line makes CI red again, which
# is the point — nothing is hidden, it is written down.
#
# RULES FOR ADDING A LINE
#   1. The secret must be verified NON-sensitive, or already rotated and dead.
#   2. Say which, and why, in the comment above the line.
#   3. A finding that is a real secret is NEVER added here — it is rotated first,
#      and the line is added only after the old value is dead.

